X. EFFECT OF SURFACE ROUGHNESS ON LAMINAR TO TURBULENT TRANSITION
The transition from laminar to turbulent flow is another area of great interest in microfluidics applications. The current results show that for all gap sizes, the transition to turbulent flow begins at Reynolds numbers in the range 18002100 for smooth channels (without any ridges). However, for the sawtooth surface, it is observed that as the relative roughness increases, the transition begins early. For relative roughness values of e/Dho=0.06-0.14, the results indicate that the transition Reynolds number based on the constricted flow diameter, Re,,f, varies between the values of 800 and 350 for air and water, respectively.
XI. CONCLUSIONS
(1) The constricted flow area is introduced as an important parameter in representing the effect of roughness on friction factor. A modified Moody diagram is presented with this parameter. The resulting friction factor based on the constricted flow area asymptotically reaches a constant value of
